  "summary": "A Texas man convicted of armed robbery has asked the Fifth Circuit to overturn his conviction, arguing that police use of Flock ALPR data to reconstruct his movements across Dallas-Fort Worth suburbs constituted a warrantless search violating the Fourth Amendment. The appeal follows an earlier Fifth Circuit ruling that single-camera ALPR use is not a Fourth Amendment violation, and parallels an Oklahoma federal ruling suppressing Flock evidence in a Tulsa County drug case.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "No mortal human has ever possessed such an ability to effortlessly and immediately reconstruct any person's movements",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock's surveillance database indiscriminately collects a vast trove of locational data about Americans who drive or ride in cars",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "We're trying to figure out whether or not we're there [in the Brownlee case], and when is the tipping point",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "ruled Oct. 1 that use of Flock data violated the Fourth Amendment in a case involving a drug arrest \u2014 seemingly the first ruling of its kind",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    }
  ],
